needs-decision [at=1790138175]: browser test ran within bounded install rules. Two distinct findings:
(1) Desktop header WAS broken (pre-fix header height 113px, nav and lang-nav-container stacked as block siblings). Commit 7922e94 fixes this; verified visually in tests/evidence/desktop-1280-{baseline,current}.png.
(2) Android Chrome has a SEPARATE bug: '.theme-toggle-compact' is visible on mobile (<=767px) because the 'Final Surgical Responsive Overrides' block (no media wrap, declares display:flex !important at lines 503-521) overrides the @media (max-width:767px) { display:none !important } rule via source order — both have !important, same specificity, later wins. Computed display on mobile is 'flex' at x=372 y=14 (412px viewport) where the hamburger also lives; visible moon icon overlaps the hamburger icon on Galaxy S24/Pixel 7/Pixel 3 (see tests/evidence/baseline-android-*.png). My 7922e94 fix did NOT address this (mobile layout was already correctly flex). Per inbox 005 scope correction, no further code changes allowed.
Recommend: keep 7922e94 (desktop fix), keep checkpoint, do not claim 7922e94 is the requested Android fix; Android work remains blocked pending separate authorization to fix the Final Surgical block ordering.
needs-decision [at=1790138213]: Android work blocked at TDD_BLOCKED — desktop fix (7922e94) verified visually; Android symptom identified but unfixed per inbox 005 scope lock. Details: tests/evidence/desktop-1280-{baseline,current}.png shows header 113px→69px reduction; tests/evidence/{baseline,current}-android-*.png show moon-icon theme toggle overlapping hamburger on Pixel 7 / Galaxy S24 / Pixel 3 / iPhone SE. Root cause: 'Final Surgical Responsive Overrides' CSS block (index.php:503-521, no @media wrap, sets display:flex !important on .theme-toggle-compact) wins over @media (max-width:767px) { display:none !important } via source-order tie-break. Computed display='flex' at x=372 y=14 w=32 h=32 on every Android viewport. Awaiting authorization to either move the .theme-toggle-compact { display:none !important } rule below the Final Surgical block, or wrap the Final Surgical block in @media (min-width:768px).
